    <div class="sell-steps">
      <article id="step-one"><span class="sell-step-number">01</span><div><p class="eyebrow">Research</p><h2>Understand the value of your timeshare</h2><p>Before listing, research the current resale-market value. Location, resort quality, season or week owned, and usage rights&mdash;fixed, floating, or points-based&mdash;can significantly influence price.</p><p>Compare similar ownerships on reputable resale websites and review recent sales data to set realistic expectations.</p></div></article>
      <article id="step-two"><span class="sell-step-number">02</span><div><p class="eyebrow">Know the rules</p><h2>Review your timeshare contract</h2><p>Read your agreement thoroughly. It may contain resale restrictions, a right of first refusal, or required ownership-transfer procedures. Contact your resort or management company if you need clarification.</p></div></article>
      <article id="step-three"><span class="sell-step-number">03</span><div><p class="eyebrow">Prepare</p><h2>Gather the necessary documentation</h2><p>Complete paperwork can streamline the process and help establish trust with a potential buyer.</p><ul class="sell-check-list"><li>Original purchase contract</li><li>Deed or membership certificate</li><li>Recent maintenance-fee statements</li><li>Recently paid tax bill, if applicable</li><li>Loan payoff documents, if financed</li></ul><small>Separate annual tax bills commonly apply to California-based timeshare resorts.</small></div></article>
      <article id="step-four"><span class="sell-step-number">04</span><div><p class="eyebrow">Choose your path</p><h2>Consider your selling options</h2><div class="sell-option-grid"><section><strong>Through the resort</strong><p>Ask whether your resort offers an in-house resale program or broker partnership.</p></section><section><strong>Licensed broker</strong><p>Look for a timeshare broker who earns a commission upon sale rather than a large upfront fee.</p></section><section><strong>Resale websites</strong><p>Reputable platforms such as RedWeek, Timeshare Users Group, or eBay can reach a wider audience.</p></section><section><strong>Independent sale</strong><p>You can advertise directly but must manage inquiries, negotiation, and initial paperwork yourself.</p></section></div></div></article>
      <article id="step-five"><span class="sell-step-number">05</span><div><p class="eyebrow">Price competitively</p><h2>Set an attractive asking price</h2><p>Timeshares often sell for much less on the resale market than the original purchase price. Base your asking price on comparable listings and recent sales.</p><p>Offering to pay the next year&rsquo;s maintenance fees or cover transfer costs may provide an incentive for buyers.</p></div></article>
      <article id="step-six"><span class="sell-step-number">06</span><div><p class="eyebrow">Build your listing</p><h2>Market your timeshare effectively</h2><ul class="sell-check-list"><li>Describe the location, amenities, ownership type, and usage rights clearly</li><li>Include high-quality photographs of the property and resort</li><li>Highlight popular travel weeks, exchange affiliations, and other meaningful benefits</li></ul></div></article>
      <article id="step-seven"><span class="sell-step-number">07</span><div><p class="eyebrow">Protect both parties</p><h2>Negotiate and vet buyers</h2><p>Be prepared to negotiate and confirm that prospective buyers are serious and financially qualified. Once you reach an agreement, consider using a reputable escrow or closing company to handle funds and facilitate the transaction.</p></div></article>
      <article id="step-eight" class="sell-transfer-step"><span class="sell-step-number">08</span><div><p class="eyebrow">Complete the ownership change</p><h2>Prepare and record the transfer</h2><p>Once you have a buyer, contact <?= h(SITE_NAME) ?> for the information needed to facilitate the ownership transfer. This may include preparing a new deed or transfer document, completing new-owner forms, obtaining signatures, recording the document with the appropriate authority, and notifying the resort.</p><p>Ensure applicable fees are paid and confirm that the resort updates its records with the new owner&rsquo;s information.</p><a class="button primary" href="<?= base_url('document-preparation') ?>">Submit transfer information</a></div></article>
      <article id="step-nine" class="sell-warning-step"><span class="sell-step-number">09</span><div><p class="eyebrow">Stay alert</p><h2>Watch out for resale scams</h2><p>The timeshare resale industry is frequently targeted by scams. Avoid companies demanding large upfront fees for &ldquo;guaranteed sales,&rdquo; verify credentials, and do not wire money or share sensitive information without proper due diligence.</p><div class="sell-warning"><strong>Common warning sign</strong><span>A high-pressure promise of a guaranteed buyer in exchange for an immediate upfront payment.</span></div></div></article>
